#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "3263f466-0901-58a7-8cd9-ed62cd3036a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5545" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb C^3,$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}i \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =i\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-3})+(-2)\\cdot \\overset{―}{4+i}+0\\cdot \\overset{―}{5} \\\\  & =i\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot (4-i)+0 \\\\  & =-3i-8+2i \\\\  & =-8-i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133496 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/id #uuid "b30bd921-7418-5a57-96eb-0c79dd5e4712" :question/math-academy-id "e-5547" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ over $\\mathbb{C}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle\\in\\mathbb R$ for any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$\n\n- $\\langle \\mathrm{i}\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle =  \\langle \\mathbf{a},  -\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$ for any  $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$\n\n- $\\langle\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a},\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number for any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument  and the antilinearity in the second argument, we have\n\n$$\n\\langle  \\mathrm{i}\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle =  \\mathrm{i}\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle  = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\overline{\\, \\mathrm{i}\\, } \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a},- \\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle\n$$\n\n \nfor any  $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument  and the antilinearity in the second argument, we have\n\n$$\n\\langle\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a},\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a} \\rangle =\\mathrm{i}\\cdot \\bar{ \\mathrm{i} }\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = -\\mathrm{i}^2\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle= \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle\n$$\n\n \nfor any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n.$ But by positive-definiteness, this is a nonnegative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133604 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/id #uuid "8d5f83f0-dfb9-5437-a0e5-f33f68a59cec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8578" :question/problem "Given that $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C},$ find $\\big\\langle -2\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}, \\: \\mathbf{c} \\big\\rangle$ if \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = 3+5\\text{i}, \\quad \\quad \\langle \\mathbf{c}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = 2-4\\text{i}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-2a+3b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨-2a,c⟩+⟨3b,c⟩ \\\\  & =-2⟨a,c⟩+3⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2⟨a,c⟩+3⟨b,c⟩ & =-2⟨a,c⟩+3\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle$ and $\\langle \\mathbf{c}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2⟨a,c⟩+3\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩} & =-2(3+5\\text{i})+3\\overset{―}{(2-4\\text{i})} \\\\  & =-2(3+5\\text{i})+3(2+4\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-6-10\\text{i}+6+12\\text{i} \\\\  & =2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e97f4a8-71d4-5054-a157-d9650fe68012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+3i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308165} {:answer/id #uuid "bae851d6-5cdf-5fc3-a0d2-3b2372585e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+3i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308166} {:answer/id #uuid "c45ad895-78cc-50d6-bb7d-2b0a021160bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308167} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e5185e-2613-5721-a246-009ff4ab41dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308168} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb59764-d8b8-5f51-91b6-0040f822e53b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e97f4a8-71d4-5054-a157-d9650fe68012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+3i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308165} :answer-field/id #uuid "4961f4eb-ee14-52ce-8703-c3bc8219c4f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7180dc99-97c3-575a-b7f9-b8879ffd2435" :question/math-academy-id "q-141039" :question/problem "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{4},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1+2i \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}2+i \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}}+{x}_{4}\\overset{―}{{y}_{4}} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\overset{―}{(2+i)}+2\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-3})+(1+2i)\\cdot \\overset{―}{1}+1\\cdot \\overset{―}{2} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (2-i)+2\\cdot (-3)+(1+2i)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(-2+i)+(-6)+(1+2\\text{i})+2 \\\\  & =-5+3i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e90e50cc-5a33-547f-8a5e-c9512c042acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 - 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132537} {:answer/id #uuid "0724d53a-3c99-5942-8ad5-e9b8d8e2227b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 + 5\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132538} {:answer/id #uuid "546294fb-7f23-5f11-9046-3972539650c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 + \\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132539} {:answer/id #uuid "58874e76-d681-5b68-a9e4-06a584e0f555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 - 5\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132540} {:answer/id #uuid "645439f0-15da-564b-8699-a2907248ebaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 - \\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "645439f0-15da-564b-8699-a2907248ebaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 - \\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132541} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a5d3a6b-d844-5418-b685-9b2c1ab44bc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21a5e3b9-3277-5fa1-a50a-f09ae68d74c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-43803" :question/problem "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{4},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2+i\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}}+{x}_{4}\\overset{―}{{y}_{4}} \\\\  & =(1+i)\\cdot \\overset{―}{2}+1\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-4})+2\\cdot \\overset{―}{6}+3\\cdot (\\overset{―}{2+\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =(1+i)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 6+3\\cdot (2-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(2+2i)+(-4)+12+(6-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =16-i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b8cdfe0-b0d8-541b-ace8-6390de59f108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11 - 4\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132543} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e5fa48-bd68-52ab-9c57-cf1bbfe67eb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132544} {:answer/id #uuid "fcadfbb4-517f-5120-8018-354a5c439e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 - 4\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132545} {:answer/id #uuid "15cbba13-b018-54ae-864c-281f2976c31e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14 + \\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132546} {:answer/id #uuid "44e3ebb7-6021-5ac5-98e3-9d027013e7bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 - 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b8cdfe0-b0d8-541b-ace8-6390de59f108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11 - 4\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132543} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d5ef943-ac4c-5829-88ba-14e24ba16a9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59f23fa6-6c50-54ee-b500-91f27bbfcf52" :question/math-academy-id "q-43804" :question/problem "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{3},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2-i\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2+i\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =(1+i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{1+i})+2\\cdot \\overset{―}{3}+(2-i)(\\overset{―}{2+\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =(1+i)\\cdot (1-i)+2\\cdot 3+(2-i)(2-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2+6+(3-4\\text{i}) \\\\  & =11-4i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "597ec12e-ee3e-5b0b-b5be-ead598162e52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308172} {:answer/id #uuid "95f8f036-cab9-5b1b-a4b2-550323d235f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308173} {:answer/id #uuid "08900b72-d37c-5f47-ad99-77fd618db622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308174} {:answer/id #uuid "af51d694-997f-52c1-bdad-a7d7402a4c5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308175} {:answer/id #uuid "27d31aa6-083a-5123-8345-74a498f33272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08900b72-d37c-5f47-ad99-77fd618db622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308174} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd51acd2-7a46-5a9f-a1f8-96e756ef2dbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "186012f8-29d6-50d1-9f77-57d63103562b" :question/math-academy-id "q-43808" :question/problem "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{3},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}i \\\\ 1+2i \\\\ -i\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ -1 \\\\ -i\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =i\\cdot (\\overset{―}{1+i})+(1+2i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-1})+(-i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =i\\cdot (1-i)+(1+2i)\\cdot (-1)+(-i)\\cdot \\text{i} \\\\  & =(1+i)+(-1-2i)+1 \\\\  & =1-i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f95a0b36-f3ae-5637-a154-b9e613dbe4cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308179} {:answer/id #uuid "af92a68f-23bd-51ca-96f1-dabda5879703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20+56i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308180} {:answer/id #uuid "be7e2dfe-e4ad-57cf-845e-8a4f3a3c5356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-27i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308181} {:answer/id #uuid "67e51f47-e0b9-55af-bb5c-293ebd01050b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-72i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308182} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3d6043-0f8a-566e-afb0-b0f3444b7231" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-55i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67e51f47-e0b9-55af-bb5c-293ebd01050b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-72i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308182} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4f42f22-20d8-53f5-b44c-3dddcfbb2075" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5127a6a5-505b-5275-893e-366617271bb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-96829" :question/problem "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{3},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1-4\\text{i} \\\\ 1-6\\text{i} \\\\ \\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}+4 \\\\ \\text{i}+9 \\\\ -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =(1-4i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{i+4})+(1-6i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{i+9})+\\text{i}\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =(1-4i)\\cdot (4-i)+(1-6i)\\cdot (9-i)+\\text{i}\\cdot \\text{i} \\\\  & =(-17i)+(3-55i)+(-1) \\\\  & =2-72i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84a54968-0216-55c2-9a8a-0bcdf4fca7f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133269} {:answer/id #uuid "ac3b910c-ed49-5562-b5b5-3c07647d2ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133270} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc36f7c-b92f-5126-9272-ffb91d12ee8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133271} {:answer/id #uuid "c6db85b6-61f2-5ef6-aaca-a1350ca39d45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133272} {:answer/id #uuid "3027af17-4f03-5cb9-83dc-c7b31c856e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac3b910c-ed49-5562-b5b5-3c07647d2ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133270} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b987768-1cf1-5774-a3e1-0eb5e6a4f663" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45f8ab8c-12d7-5df5-b151-ee8250c362bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-96833" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is false. To factor out a constant multiple from the second argument, we must change it to its complex conjugate.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5a96f74-d810-5102-837c-d10677244aa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308186} {:answer/id #uuid "627e235f-736a-57a0-8c4c-b927d8a0a834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308187} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed76953-1190-5937-83a2-bdf61fdead78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308188} {:answer/id #uuid "e55e8215-30fc-5030-967d-854ae6eaffb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308189} {:answer/id #uuid "4eeaef49-611b-536d-b874-5f989b63e2b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ed76953-1190-5937-83a2-bdf61fdead78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308188} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c239fd8-ff0e-5201-985f-8ef8c181523a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d07c4830-0431-5d0d-83f4-02741718f7fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-97149" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,a⟩>0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $a=0$ then $⟨a,a⟩=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the inner product in ${C}^{n}$ is linear in the first argument. So, a constant multiple from the first argument can be factored out.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40d58baa-e21b-5238-bfbc-4b8a220e69f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308193} {:answer/id #uuid "c7db8835-10e4-594f-8c15-e31889afbcbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308194} {:answer/id #uuid "e62994ca-1b05-5590-96bb-9d47fecb5e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15-10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308195} {:answer/id #uuid "e69e99b6-7a4a-5d21-9ac4-0af246639467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308196} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c71d9f-f467-5a00-a274-0f740c6285e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e62994ca-1b05-5590-96bb-9d47fecb5e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15-10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308195} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8b895dc-840f-5c35-a946-a03d2571ca15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d2a3e3d-fac2-529b-bde6-b9bc5f4517f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-97159" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,6b+c⟩$ if   \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=2+3\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=3+8\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,6b+c⟩ & =⟨a,6b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{6}⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =6⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ & =6\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩ & =6(\\overset{―}{2+3\\text{i}})+(3+8\\text{i}) \\\\  & =6(2-3\\text{i})+(3+8\\text{i}) \\\\  & =12-18\\text{i}+3+8\\text{i} \\\\  & =15-10\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4f4a541-cdc7-5060-beeb-a81e44be5dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308200} {:answer/id #uuid "1b6e2158-aa94-5ada-a88f-723c00825432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308201} {:answer/id #uuid "deb2588b-8739-5da8-8810-f242c07619d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308202} {:answer/id #uuid "412eb91b-8576-5523-b2c5-ad229256b0a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308203} {:answer/id #uuid "73709385-4a47-5c43-ad5b-6fda12735a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b6e2158-aa94-5ada-a88f-723c00825432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308201} :answer-field/id #uuid "318b4b7f-23b7-5ce7-8ad1-8635e229e78b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "16657068-2558-5f9e-bf6b-f53d86050fca" :question/math-academy-id "q-97180" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,b+(2+5\\text{i})c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=3-\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,b+(2+5\\text{i})c⟩ & =⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,(2+5\\text{i})c⟩ \\\\  & =⟨a,b⟩+(\\overset{―}{2+5\\text{i}})⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =⟨a,b⟩+(2-5\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,b⟩+(2-5\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+(2-5\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+(2-5\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =\\overset{―}{3-\\text{i}}+(2-5\\text{i})\\cdot \\text{i} \\\\  & =3+\\text{i}+(2-5\\text{i})\\cdot \\text{i} \\\\  & =3+\\text{i}+2\\text{i}-5(-1) \\\\  & =8+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff7fcd72-7b6a-5156-a3c9-4a1862e148ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308207} {:answer/id #uuid "85e026bc-5a06-57fe-a39d-4415f3110507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308208} {:answer/id #uuid "43cad395-6f95-5566-b226-feaea161f645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308209} {:answer/id #uuid "c5dd3b3f-18c7-52ce-8a75-3b6277acdb3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308210} {:answer/id #uuid "d92fd167-4e5b-5f69-bf4e-f278d70e519c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43cad395-6f95-5566-b226-feaea161f645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308209} :answer-field/id #uuid "8136771c-6f38-55d7-8461-51b6d50d5644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9dd1eb6a-d2e0-570d-98d8-cddfe7919baf" :question/math-academy-id "q-97193" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,(4-\\text{i})b+c⟩$ if  \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=-1-\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,(4-\\text{i})b+c⟩ & =⟨a,(4-\\text{i})b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =(\\overset{―}{4-\\text{i}})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =(4+\\text{i})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+\\text{i})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c\\text{ }⟩ & =(4+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩ & =(4+\\text{i})\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-1-\\text{i}})+5 \\\\  & =(4+\\text{i})\\cdot (-1+\\text{i})+5 \\\\  & =-5+3\\text{i}+5 \\\\  & =3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf518d7a-01f5-50d2-b94f-65e8a488c869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308214} {:answer/id #uuid "49b4ef0e-f1e5-54ed-ba18-bbe54250378c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308215} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f74b48-910b-58f3-8e92-bba56a87d7ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308216} {:answer/id #uuid "7719dd6e-1c7d-57be-820b-737fe5ab28e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308217} {:answer/id #uuid "9344d692-5295-53a1-bdc1-184b52e08273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9344d692-5295-53a1-bdc1-184b52e08273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308218} :answer-field/id #uuid "e34b21cc-e704-5a61-9eb7-a3fda73d7473" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa324a2f-2ed0-54ed-bd7c-536b84e60017" :question/math-academy-id "q-97209" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨4a-b,\\,c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨c,a⟩=1-2\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨b,c⟩=2+3\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨4a-b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨4a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4⟨a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩ & =4\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}-⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨c,a⟩$ and $⟨b,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}-⟨b,c⟩ & =4\\overset{―}{(1-2\\text{i})}-(2+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4(1+2\\text{i})-(2+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4+8\\text{i}-2-3\\text{i} \\\\  & =2+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff61cd9-1ab9-54da-985c-eefda8afbcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8-12\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308221} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee09841-ed7c-52fd-a1d1-e33781fa5cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+12\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308222} {:answer/id #uuid "abed3f46-9bbc-5fa9-98cd-3999d7a7e37e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308223} {:answer/id #uuid "f025f391-8994-5de9-83cd-c7de8ad63ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308224} {:answer/id #uuid "db1313f3-9453-575a-b454-ecf9b7fde1e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ff61cd9-1ab9-54da-985c-eefda8afbcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8-12\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308221} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf0700f2-8363-5aac-9ec1-f258333daceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0668cc2a-2453-5ec6-b822-8bc3dff7632f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97215" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨6a+4b,\\,c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨c,a⟩=2+4\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨c,b⟩=-5-3\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6a+4b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨6a,c⟩+⟨4b,c⟩ \\\\  & =6⟨a,c⟩+4⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6⟨a,c⟩+4⟨b,c⟩ & =6\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+4\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨c,a⟩$ and $⟨c,b⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+4\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩} & =6(\\overset{―}{2+4\\text{i}})+4\\overset{―}{(-5-3\\text{i})} \\\\  & =6(2-4\\text{i})+4(-5+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =12-24\\text{i}-20+12\\text{i} \\\\  & =-8-12\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78ae8a6e-64c5-5c6d-9246-4ac0eb3d4eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308228} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f96905-f6cd-53ad-88cd-7c8551c2948d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308229} {:answer/id #uuid "f0551a2f-4a60-58cb-a598-26b021d36a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308230} {:answer/id #uuid "dafb893a-2fed-57f4-b32e-01a16b0f6572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308231} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb83b1e-f1a8-563d-b597-1ea16704e11d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0551a2f-4a60-58cb-a598-26b021d36a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308230} :answer-field/id #uuid "84213374-f1b5-54a9-ba3b-3c509c124fda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5739baad-8a41-53ea-98cf-d058e0e17b20" :question/math-academy-id "q-97248" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,-ia⟩=-i⟨a,a⟩$ for any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, by the antilinearity in the second argument we have\n\n$$\n⟨a,-ia⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\ne -i⟨a,a⟩\n$$\n\n \nfor any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5381d251-9885-5c9e-a699-72f24285757a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308235} {:answer/id #uuid "577b9a0c-91bd-56d1-af63-ec819078319d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308236} {:answer/id #uuid "48e0a8b7-7260-5a55-b4d8-0d92c52c3651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308237} {:answer/id #uuid "845d7f5f-8b5c-5dc3-9a52-ac98c1f2061a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308238} {:answer/id #uuid "cc573856-49c5-5193-a781-d378667321ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "577b9a0c-91bd-56d1-af63-ec819078319d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308236} :answer-field/id #uuid "effef674-0dac-5630-a8b8-2d53c8518066" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c55ff0e-5512-5272-a32d-49392f4f28c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-97258" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨a,b⟩=⟨b,a⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$  $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number.\n\n- Statement II is false. To factor out a constant multiple from the second argument, we must change it to its complex conjugate.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4afe5cc5-6a3f-5f9c-a3fb-fa76e06d93ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308242} {:answer/id #uuid "74419b3d-b13f-57c7-9e9f-07c716d35a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308243} {:answer/id #uuid "b3460bc8-25ee-503c-8066-b2decb3a421a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308244} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc0aed1-8969-50df-80c0-ac150ad1f5db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308245} {:answer/id #uuid "da903bef-5d47-524e-b08e-ec3f5446ac3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da903bef-5d47-524e-b08e-ec3f5446ac3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308246} :answer-field/id #uuid "49e42b05-211a-582f-9fa8-59379f469326" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6174044c-c49a-50d3-8871-9332394846b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-97296" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨a,b⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $i⟨a,b⟩=⟨a,-ib⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- There is vector $a\\in {C}^{n}$ such that  $⟨a,a⟩<0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. A complex number is equal to its conjugate if and only if it is a real number. The inner product over $C$  is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,-ib⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false.  The inner product over $C$ is positive-definite. So, for any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
